## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of code-splitting, lazy-loading, and performance budgets in a real frontend workflow. It also checks how to gate heavy modules and verify budgets with CI.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic import() for code-splitting
- IntersectionObserver/interaction gate for lazy-loading
- Skeleton UI for perceived performance
- Service Worker caching strategy for loaded modules
- Lighthouse budgets and CI verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// Gate wrapper to lazy-load AuditTrailPanel on visibility
import React, { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';

export function AuditGate({ onLoad }) {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  const [loaded, setLoaded] = useState(false);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!ref.current) return;
    const obs = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      if (entries[0].isIntersecting) {
        import('./AuditTrailPanel').then((mod) => {
          onLoad && onLoad(mod.default);
          setLoaded(true);
        });
        obs.disconnect();
      }
    });
    obs.observe(ref.current);
    return () => obs.disconnect();
  }, [onLoad]);
  return <div ref={ref} aria-label="AuditGate" style={{ minHeight: 20 }} />;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle SSR compatibility with this gate?
- What metrics or Lighthouse CI steps ensure the budget stays intact across deployments?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design performance-sensitive React architectures handling high-frequency data, including state shaping, rendering discipline, and UX consistency under load.

## Key Concepts

- Memoized selectors and reselect
- Data virtualization (react-window) for large series
- Rendering optimizations with React.memo and useSelector shallowEqual
- Concurrent UI patterns with useTransition
- Context isolation to prevent unnecessary rerenders

## Code Example

```javascript
import { createSelector } from 'reselect';

// Input: state.metrics.stream = Array<{t: number, v: number}>
const bucketSelector = (state) => state.metrics.stream;

export const selectLast1sBuckets = createSelector(
  bucketSelector,
  (stream) => {
    const now = Date.now();
    const oneSec = 1000;
    const buckets = {};
    for (const p of stream) {
      const bucket = Math.floor((now - p.t) / oneSec);
      buckets[bucket] = (buckets[bucket] || 0) + p.v;
    }
    // return as ordered array of {k,v}
    return Object.keys(buckets).sort((a,b)=>a-b).map(k=>({t: Number(k), v: buckets[k]}));
  }
);
```

